-- title:
--   Theorem 3.1 — a Feynman–Kac representation for the nonlocal problem
-- statement:
--   Under the assumptions of §2, the terminal-boundary value problem
--
--   $$
--   -\frac{\partial W}{\partial t}(t,x)-\mathcal LW(t,x)=f(t,x)\ \text{ in }Q,\qquad W(t,x)=h(t,x)+\mathcal BW(t,x)\ \text{ on }\partial_pQ
--   $$
--
--   has a unique bounded solution $W\in C^{1,2}(Q)\cap C(\bar Q\setminus D)$, which for all $(t,x)\in\bar Q$ can be represented as
--
--   $$
--   W(t,x)=E_t^x\Big[\int_t^\infty e^{-r(s-t)}\Big(\prod_{t\le\theta_j<s}\bar\nu(v_{\theta_j-},X_{\theta_j-})\Big)f(v_{s-},X_{s-})\,ds+\sum_{\theta_i\ge t}e^{-r(\theta_i-t)}\Big(\prod_{t\le\theta_j<\theta_i}\bar\nu(v_{\theta_j-},X_{\theta_j-})\Big)h(v_{\theta_i-},X_{\theta_i-})\Big].
--   $$
--
--   Here $(v,X)$ is the diffusion with random jumps from $\partial_pQ$, started from $v_{t-}=t$, $X_{t-}=x$. The solution is the expected discounted cash flow of a particle that loses a fraction of its mass at each jump.
--
--   The Lean statement has four clauses: existence of a solution; agreement of every solution with it on $\bar Q$; existence of a realization from every point of $\bar Q$; and, in every realization, integrability of the payoff with expectation $W(t,x)$. At $(t,x)\in\partial_pQ$ the realization has $\theta_1=t$, the convention of p.10.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - encoding: bounded smooth domain → open, connected, bounded, with a global $C^\infty$ defining function $\rho$; reason: equivalent for bounded domains; the paper gives no degree of smoothness, read as $C^\infty$
--   - encoding: $f$ uniformly Hölder on $\bar Q$ with exponent $\alpha$ → Hölder for the metric of $\mathbb R\times\mathbb R^d$ with an existential $\alpha\in(0,1]$; reason: with $\alpha$ existential this and the parabolic Hölder class coincide on the bounded set $\bar Q$
--   - reading: $d$-dimensional balls, open or closed not specified → open balls; reason: the two readings agree, since $\varepsilon$ is arbitrary and balls can be slightly enlarged
--   - reading: measurability of $(t,x)\mapsto\nu_{t,x}$ with respect to the weak convergence topology → Borel for the topology of `FiniteMeasure` on $Q$; reason: this is the direct meaning of the phrase
--   - encoding: the process $(v,X)$ with $\theta_{i+1}=\inf\{s>\theta_i: X_{s-}\in\partial\mathcal O\text{ or }v_{s-}=T\}$ on a fixed filtered space → any `Realization`: the filtered space is part of the data, $\theta_{i+1}$ is the end of a cycle in $Q$ with left limit in $\partial_pQ$, stochastic integrals are `HasItoIntegral`, the conditional law is given $\mathcal F_{\theta_i-}$ with $\mathcal F_{\tau-}$ generated by the sets $A\cap\{s<\tau\}$; reason: same object: first hit written without an infimum of a possibly empty set; Mathlib has no $\mathcal F_{\tau-}$
--   - encoding: $f(v_{s-},X_{s-})$ and products over $\theta_j$ in $Y$ → $f(v_s,X_s)$ under $ds$ and `finprod`; reason: the left limit differs from the value at jump times only, a Lebesgue null set; only finitely many $\theta_j$ lie below $s$ almost surely
--   - generalization: $|h(s,x)|\le K_h\cdot s$, (2.16) → $|h(s,x)|\le K_h\cdot T$ on $\partial_pQ$; reason: (2.16) implies this bound because $s\le T$, and the proofs use only this bound (p.23)
--   - encoding: the expectation in the statement → integrability of the functional is asserted explicitly; reason: the paper's expectation presupposes it; without it a Bochner integral defaults to $0$
--   - encoding: "unique" → every solution agrees with $W$ on $\bar Q$; reason: values off $\bar Q$ are not part of a solution
--   - encoding: $E_t^x$ on the paper's construction → the expectation under every `Realization` started at $(t,t,x)$, one of which exists; reason: the filtered space is part of the data, and the existence clause keeps the representation from being vacuous
-- source:
--   Dai, Kou, Yang, A Stochastic Representation for Nonlocal Parabolic PDEs with Applications, SSRN 3541591, Theorem 3.1, p.11; eq. (1.1)-(1.3), (1.6), pp.1-2

import Definitions.Def_NonlocalFK_Realization

open MeasureTheory Set Filter

universe u

namespace NonlocalFK

/-- Theorem 3.1 (A Feynman-Kac Representation) (Dai, Kou, Yang, A Stochastic Representation for
Nonlocal Parabolic PDEs with Applications, SSRN 3541591, p.11): the terminal-boundary value problem
(1.1)–(1.2) has a unique bounded solution `W ∈ C^{1,2}(Q) ∩ C(Q̄ ∖ D)`, which can be represented as
(1.6) for all `(t, x) ∈ Q̄`.

**Formalization Note.** Uniqueness is agreement on `Q̄`. For every `(t, x) ∈ Q̄` a realization of the
process started at `(t, t, x)` exists, so the representation is not vacuous, and in every such
realization the payoff of (1.6) is well defined and integrable with expectation `W(t, x)`. At
`(t, x) ∈ ∂ₚQ` the realization has `θ₁ = t`, the convention of p.10. The bound `|h| ≤ Kₕ T` stands
for (2.16). -/
theorem theorem_3_1 {d : ℕ} (m : Model d) (hS : m.Standing) (h22 : m.Assumption22) {Kh : ℝ}
    (hG : m.Growth Kh) :
    ∃ W : ℝ × E d → ℝ, m.IsSolution W ∧
      (∀ W' : ℝ × E d → ℝ, m.IsSolution W' → EqOn W' W m.Qbar) ∧
      (∀ p ∈ m.Qbar, ∃ (Ω : Type u) (_ : MeasurableSpace Ω),
        Nonempty (Realization m p.1 p.1 p.2 Ω)) ∧
      ∀ p ∈ m.Qbar, ∀ (Ω : Type u) [MeasurableSpace Ω] (R : Realization m p.1 p.1 p.2 Ω),
        R.PayoffIntegrable ∧ W p = ∫ ω, R.payoff ω ∂(R.P) := by sorry

end NonlocalFK
